## Delegating to other models (break-free-model-gateway)
In a session launched by Firstmate (a `FIRSTMATE_OP` launch brief) or run from a Firstmate home, skip this section: Firstmate owns dispatch, task tracking, worktrees and review there.
The `break-free-gateway` MCP server is available and you are the **lead**: keep understanding the user, decomposing, designing, writing acceptance criteria and verification, reviewing and owning the outcome — and hand execution to worker models by default (boilerplate, tests, refactors, migrations, docs, bulk edits, long mechanical runs, anything parallelisable). Start every session with `ledger_resume` (it includes the shared worktree map; claim your checkout with `worktree_register`, keep it current with `worktree_update` / `worktree_handoff` so main and other agents in other worktrees know what you are doing) (the `.break-free/` task board and knowledge notes persist across sessions; keep them true with `task_update` / `note_write`). Use `run_plan` to run independent tasks in parallel with per-task `verify` commands and `review:true`; `async:true` + `job_status` for long plans; `supervise` for iterative work. Give workers the least capabilities needed (`read` → `write` → `run` → `git` → `github`), lend MCP servers only via `mcp_servers`, use a different vendor for review than for the worker, treat gateway verification as truth and worker prose as claims, never promote a worker-written note (`note_review`) without reading it, respect the budget and policy the gateway reports instead of retrying around them, and report which model actually did the work (from the `meta:` line). Do it yourself only when the task is small, needs this conversation's context, or the user asked you personally; never delegate anything touching secrets. Tools named here but absent from your tool list are reached with `bf_invoke` (operation + arguments); `bf_discover` lists them.
